{
  "image_name": "0bd4cbed90b83630994dcf18beab6be6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several designs for integrating light harvesting with electrochemical energy storage. In all cases, a liquid electrolyte sits between an anode and cathode, while ions move through the solution and electrons travel through an external circuit. The basic idea is that illumination creates electron–hole pairs in a photoactive electrode or attached photovoltaic layer, and these charges drive ion migration so the device can be charged by light and later discharge to power a load.\n\nThe comparison shows different architectures. One uses a separate photovoltaic part connected electrically to a conventional storage cell, so sunlight generates electrons externally while cations move across the electrolyte to store energy. Another places the photovoltaic element directly above the storage electrode, making a more compact stacked device. A third concept uses a photoactive cathode itself, where light excites the cathode and enables charging without a separate solar cell. The last panel highlights a rechargeable photo-battery behavior: under “photocharge,” light drives charge separation and ion redistribution, and during “discharge” the stored energy is released through an external circuit to light a bulb.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes how solar conversion and ion-based storage can be combined in different structural arrangements.",
